</graph>

<section><title>Slope vectors in plot of <m>w</m> versus time</title>

<p>When creating a slope vector in the bottom panel for each of the vectors you drew in the top panel, remember that you can move the test point near a corresponding value of <m>w(t)</m> to get a gauge the appropriate slope of the vector.</p>


<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolVecs1" type="boolean" target="$showSolVecs1" newValue="true" disabled="$showSolVecs1">
  <label>show vector</label>
</updateValue> to draw a slope vector in the interval where <m>w &lt; $a</m>.
<answer disabled="not $showSolVecs1" name="ansSolVec1">
  <award matchPartial="true">
    <when>
      <boolean name="sv1Location">$wt.copy &lt; $a</boolean>
      and
      <boolean name="sv1Direction">$sv1Location and $wt.v1r and $wt.v1m > 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$sv1Direction and 0.1 &lt; $wt.v1m &lt; 1</boolean>
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.copy $wt.vector1Disp $wt.v1m
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec1" condition="not $sv1Location">
  The vector must start in the interval where <m>w &lt; $a</m>.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec1" condition="$sv1Location and not $wt.v1r">
  The vector must point rightward, as it should point in the direction where time is increasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec1" condition="$sv1Location and $wt.v1r and not $sv1Direction">
  Since <m>w(t)</m> is increasing in this interval, the vector must angle upward.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec1" condition="$sv1Direction and $wt.v1m &lt;= 0.1">
  Increase the upward angle slightly to make it more noticeable.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec1" condition="$sv1Direction and $wt.v1m >= 1">
  The upward angle shouldn't be so steep.
</feedback>



<p hide="$ansSolVec1.creditAchieved = 1">(<em>Correctly place the first slope vector to unlock the second slope vector.</em>)</p>

<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolVecs2" type="boolean" target="$showSolVecs2" newValue="true" disabled="$ansSolVec1.creditAchieved &lt; 1 or $showSolVecs2">
  <label>show second vector</label>
</updateValue> to draw a vector in the bottom third of the interval <m>$a &lt; w &lt; $b</m>.
<answer disabled="not $showSolVecs2" name="ansSolVec2">
  <award matchPartial="true">
    <when>
      <boolean name="sv2Location">$a &lt; $wt.v2ty &lt; $ab1</boolean>
      and
      <boolean name="sv2Direction">$sv2Location and $wt.v2r and $wt.v2m &lt; 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$sv2Direction and  -1.6 &lt; $wt.v2m &lt; -0.3</boolean>
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.v2ty $wt.vector2Disp $wt.v2m
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec2" condition="not $sv2Location">
  The vector must start in the lower third of the interval ($a, $b), i.e., in the interval ($a, $ab1).
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec2" condition="$sv2Location and not $wt.v2r">
  The vector must point rightward, as it should point in the direction where time is increasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec2" condition="$sv2Location and $wt.v2r and not $sv2Direction">
  The vector must angle downward.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec2" condition="$sv2Direction and -0.3 &lt;= $wt.v2m">
  Increase the downward angle slightly to make it more noticeable.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec2" condition="$sv2Direction and $wt.v2m &lt;= -1.6">
  The downward angle should be less steep.
</feedback>


<p hide="$ansSolVec2.creditAchieved = 1">(<em>Correctly place the second slope vector to unlock the third slope vector.</em>)</p>


<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolVecs3" type="boolean" target="$showSolVecs3" newValue="true" disabled="$ansSolVec2.creditAchieved &lt; 1 or $showSolVecs3">
  <label>show third vector</label>
</updateValue> to draw a vector in the middle third of the interval <m>$a &lt; w &lt; $b</m>.
<answer disabled="not $showSolVecs3" name="ansSolVec3">
  <award matchPartial="true">
    <when>
      <boolean name="sv3Location">$ab1 &lt; $wt.v3ty &lt; $ab2</boolean>
      and
      <boolean name="sv3Direction">$sv3Location and $wt.v3r and $wt.v3m &lt; 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$sv3Direction and  -4 &lt; $wt.v3m &lt; -2</boolean>
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.v3ty $wt.vector3Disp $wt.v3m
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec3" condition="not $sv3Location">
  The vector must start in the middle third of the interval ($a, $b), i.e., in the interval ($ab1, $ab2).
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec3" condition="$sv3Location and not $wt.v3r">
  The vector must point rightward, as it should point in the direction where time is increasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec3" condition="$sv3Location and $wt.v3r and not $sv3Direction">
  The vector must angle downward.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec3" condition="$sv3Direction and -2 &lt;= $wt.v3m">
  Increase the downward angle.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec3" condition="$sv3Direction and $wt.v3m &lt;= -4">
  The downward angle should be less steep.
</feedback>



<p hide="$ansSolVec3.creditAchieved = 1">(<em>Correctly place the third slope vector to unlock the fourth slope vector.</em>)</p>

<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolVecs4" type="boolean" target="$showSolVecs4" newValue="true" disabled="$ansSolVec3.creditAchieved &lt; 1 or $showSolVecs4">
  <label>show fourth vector</label>
</updateValue> to draw a vector in the upper third of the interval <m>$a &lt; w &lt; $b</m>.
<answer disabled="not $showSolVecs4" name="ansSolVec4">
  <award matchPartial="true">
    <when>
      <boolean name="sv4Location">$ab2 &lt; $wt.v4ty &lt; $b</boolean>
      and
      <boolean name="sv4Direction">$sv4Location and $wt.v4r and $wt.v4m &lt; 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$sv4Direction and -1.6 &lt; $wt.v4m &lt; -0.2</boolean>
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.v4ty $wt.vector4Disp $wt.v4m
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec4" condition="not $sv4Location">
  The vector must start in the upper third of the interval ($a, $b), i.e., in the interval ($ab2, $b).
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec4" condition="$sv4Location and not $wt.v4r">
  The vector must point rightward, as it should point in the direction where time is increasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec4" condition="$sv4Location and $wt.v4r and not $sv4Direction">
  The vector must angle downward.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec4" condition="$sv4Direction and -0.2 &lt;= $wt.v4m">
  Increase the downward angle slightly to make it more noticeable.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec4" condition="$sv4Direction and $wt.v4m &lt;= -1.6">
  The downward angle should be less steep.
</feedback>

<p hide="$ansSolVec4.creditAchieved = 1">(<em>Correctly place the fourth slope vector to unlock the fifth slope vector.</em>)</p>

<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolVecs5" type="boolean" target="$showSolVecs5" newValue="true" disabled="$ansSolVec4.creditAchieved &lt; 1 or $showSolVecs5">
  <label>show fifth vector</label>
</updateValue> to draw a vector in the interval <m>$b &lt; w &lt; $c</m>.
<answer disabled="not $showSolVecs5" name="ansSolVec5">
  <award matchPartial="true">
    <when>
      <boolean name="sv5Location">$b &lt; $wt.copy1 &lt; $c</boolean>
      and
      <boolean name="sv5Direction">$sv5Location and $wt.v5r and $wt.v5m > 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$sv5Direction and 0.1 &lt; $wt.v5m &lt; 0.6</boolean>
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.copy1 $wt.vector5Disp $wt.v5m
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec5" condition="not $sv5Location">
  The vector must start in the interval ($b, $c).
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec5" condition="$sv5Location and not $wt.v5r">
  The vector must point rightward, as it should point in the direction where time is increasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec5" condition="$sv5Location and $wt.v5r and not $sv5Direction">
  The vector must angle upward.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec5" condition="$sv5Direction and 0.1 >= $wt.v5m">
  Increase the upward angle slightly to make it more noticeable.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec5" condition="$sv5Direction and $wt.v5m >= 0.6">
  The upward angle should be less steep.
</feedback>

<p hide="$ansSolVec5.creditAchieved = 1">(<em>Correctly place the fifth slope vector to unlock the sixth slope vector.</em>)</p>

<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolVecs6" type="boolean" target="$showSolVecs6" newValue="true" disabled="$ansSolVec5.creditAchieved &lt; 1 or $showSolVecs6">
  <label>show sixth vector</label>
</updateValue> to draw a vector in the interval <m>w > $c</m>.
<answer disabled="not $showSolVecs6" name="ansSolVec6">
  <award matchPartial="true">
    <when>
      <boolean name="sv6Location">$wt.copy2 > $c</boolean>
      and
      <boolean name="sv6Direction">$sv6Location and $wt.v6r and $wt.v6m &lt; 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$sv6Direction and -1 &lt; $wt.v6m &lt; -0.2</boolean>
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.copy2 $wt.vector6Disp $wt.v6m
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec6" condition="not $sv6Location">
  The vector must start in the interval where <m>w > $c</m>.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec6" condition="$sv6Location and not $wt.v6r">
  The vector must point rightward, as it should point in the direction where time is increasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec6" condition="$sv6Location and $wt.v6r and not $sv6Direction">
  The vector must angle downward.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec6" condition="$sv6Direction and -0.2 &lt;= $wt.v6m">
  Increase the downward angle slightly to make it more noticeable.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec6" condition="$sv6Direction and $wt.v6m &lt;= -1">
  The downward angle should be less steep.
</feedback>

<p hide="$ansSolVec6.creditAchieved = 1">(<em>Correctly place the sixth slope vector to unlock the last slope vectors.</em>)</p>

<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolVecs789" type="boolean" target="$showSolVecs789" newValue="true" disabled="$ansSolVec6.creditAchieved &lt; 1 or $showSolVecs789">
  <label>show last vectors</label>
</updateValue> to draw vectors at the equilibria.
<answer disabled="not $showSolVecs789" name="ansSolVec789">
  <award matchPartial="true">
    <when>
      <mathList name="vec789tails">$wt.v7ty $wt.v8ty $wt.v9ty</mathList> = <mathList name="theEs" unordered="true">$a $b $c</mathList>
      and
      <boolean>$wt.v7r and $wt.v7m = 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$wt.v8r and $wt.v8m = 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$wt.v9r and $wt.v9m = 0</boolean>
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.v7ty $wt.vector7Disp $wt.v7m
    $wt.v8ty $wt.vector8Disp $wt.v8m
    $wt.v9ty $wt.vector9Disp $wt.v9m
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec789" condition="$vec789tails != $theEs">
  The vectors must start at the equilibria <m>w=$a</m>, <m>w=$b</m>, and <m>w=$c</m>.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec789" condition="not ($wt.v7r and $wt.v8r and $wt.v9r)">
  The vectors must point rightward, as they should point in the direction where time is increasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSolVec789" condition="($wt.v7r and $wt.v7m != 0) or ($wt.v8r and $wt.v8m != 0) or ($wt.v9r and $wt.v9m != 0)">
  Since <m>w(t)</m> is not changing, the vectors must point straight to the right.
</feedback>


<p hide="$ansSolVec789.creditAchieved = 1">(<em>Correctly place all the slope vectors to unlock the the shadow vectors.</em>)</p>

<p>Click 
<updateValue name="revealShadowVecs" type="boolean" target="$showShadowVecs" newValue="true" disabled="$ansSolVec789.creditAchieved &lt; 1 or $showShadowVecs">
  <label>show shadow vectors</label>
</updateValue> (and wait because it is slow...)
to reveal copies of the slope vectors you drew shifted in time.</p>

</section>

<section><title>Drawing the solution</title>


<p hide="$showShadowVecs">(<em>Show the shadow vectors using the above button to unlock plotting solutions.</em>)</p>

<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolution1" type="boolean" target="$showSolution1" newValue="true" disabled="$showSolution1 or not $showShadowVecs">
  <label>show first solution curve</label>
</updateValue> adjust the curve so that it begins at the initial condition <m>w(0) = 2</m> and starts moving with the right slope.
<answer disabled="not $showSolution1" name="ansSol1Slope1">
  <award referencesAreResponses="$wt.p11y" matchPartial="true">
    <when>$wt.p11y = 2 and -0.6 &lt; $wt.p11m &lt; -0.1</when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.P11disp
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<!-- <p>$(wt/p11y)</p>
<p>$(wt/p11m)</p> -->
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol1Slope1" condition="$wt.p11y != 2">
  The initial point is not in the correct location.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol1Slope1" condition="$wt.p11y = 2 and $wt.p11m >= 0">
  Given that <m>w(0)=2</m>, the solution must begin by decreasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol1Slope1" condition="$wt.p11y = 2 and 0 > $wt.p11m >= -0.1">
  Increase the magnitude of the initial slope to make it easier to see that the solution begins by decreasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol1Slope1" condition="$wt.p11y = 2 and $wt.p11m &lt;= -0.6">
  The solution stating at <m>w(0)=2</m> begins by decreasing slowly.  Decrease the magnitude of the initial slope to match.
</feedback>


<p>Move the right point on the curve and set its slope so that the solution curve <m>w(t)</m> has the proper behavior for large values of <m>t</m>.
<answer disabled="not $showSolution1" name="ansSol1">
  <award>
    <when>
      $wt.p11y = 2 and -0.6 &lt; $wt.p11m &lt; -0.1
      and $wt.p12y = $a
      and $wt.p12dy = 0
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.P12 $wt.P12disp
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol1" condition="not ($wt.p11y = 2 and -0.6 &lt; $wt.p11m &lt; -0.1)">
  The initial point and slope are not correct.  First, set up up the beginning of the curve before adjusting the second point.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol1" condition="$wt.p11y = 2 and -0.6 &lt; $wt.p11m &lt; -0.1 and $wt.p12dy > 0">
  Can the solution <m>w(t)</m> keep decreasing to smaller and smaller values?
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol1" condition="$wt.p11y = 2 and -0.6 &lt; $wt.p11m &lt; -0.1 and $wt.p12dy &lt; 0">
  Can the solution <m>w(t)</m> starting at <m>w(0) = 2</m> turn around and start increasing?
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol1" condition="$wt.p11y = 2 and -0.6 &lt; $wt.p11m &lt; -0.1 and $wt.p12dy = 0 and $wt.p12y != $a">
  The solution should asymptote by approaching what value?
</feedback>

<aside hide="$ansSol1.creditAchieved &lt; 1" collapsible="false" boxed="true">
  <title>Solution correct!</title>

  <p>We've started an animation illustrating the value of <m>w(t)</m> changing with time. </p>

  <p><updateValue name="hideAnimation1" type="boolean" target="$animation1On" newValue="false" hide="not $animation1On">
    <label>Hide animation</label>
  </updateValue>
  <updateValue name="showAnimation1" type="boolean" target="$animation1On" newValue="true" hide="$animation1On">
  <label>Show animation</label>
  </updateValue>
  </p>
</aside>

<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolution2" type="boolean" target="$showSolution2" newValue="true" disabled="$showSolution2">
  <label>show second solution curve</label> 
</updateValue> and adjust the curve so that it begins at the initial condition <m>w(0) = 4</m> and starts moving with the right slope.
<answer disabled="not $showSolution2" name="ansSol2Slope1">
  <award referencesAreResponses="$wt.p21y" matchPartial="true">
    <when>$wt.p21y = 4 and 0.1 &lt; $wt.p21m &lt; 0.4</when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.P21disp
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<!-- <p>$(wt/p21y)</p>
<p>$(wt/p21m)</p> -->
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol2Slope1" condition="$wt.p21y != 4">
  The initial point is not in the correct location.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol2Slope1" condition="$wt.p21y = 4 and $wt.p21m &lt;= 0">
  Given that <m>w(0)=4</m>, the solution must begin by increasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol2Slope1" condition="$wt.p21y = 4 and 0 &lt; $wt.p21m &lt;= 0.1">
  Increase the magnitude of the initial slope to make it easier to see that the solution begins by increasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol2Slope1" condition="$wt.p21y = 4 and $wt.p21m >= 0.4">
  Decrease the magnitude of the initial slope.
</feedback>


<p>Move the right point on the curve and set its slope so that the solution curve <m>w(t)</m> has the proper behavior for large values of <m>t</m>.
<answer disabled="not $showSolution2" name="ansSol2">
  <award>
    <when>
      $wt.p21y = 4 and 0.1 &lt; $wt.p21m &lt; 0.4
      and $wt.p22y = $c
      and $wt.p22dy = 0
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.P22 $wt.P22disp
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol2" condition="not ($wt.p21y = 4 and 0.1 &lt; $wt.p21m &lt; 0.4)">
  The initial point and slope are not correct.  First, set up up the beginning of the curve before adjusting the second point.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol2" condition="$wt.p21y = 4 and 0.1 &lt; $wt.p21m &lt; 0.4 and $wt.p22dy > 0">
  Can the solution <m>w(t)</m> starting at <m>w(0) = 4</m> turn around and start decreasing?
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol2" condition="$wt.p21y = 4 and 0.1 &lt; $wt.p21m &lt; 0.4 and $wt.p22dy &lt; 0">
  Can the solution <m>w(t)</m> keep increasing to larger and larger values?
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol2" condition="$wt.p21y = 4 and 0.1 &lt; $wt.p21m &lt; 0.4 and $wt.p22dy = 0 and $wt.p22y != $c">
  The solution should asymptote by approaching what value?
</feedback>


<aside hide="$ansSol2.creditAchieved &lt; 1" collapsible="false" boxed="true">
  <title>Another solution correct!</title>

  <p>Great, another one conquered!  We've started an animation in both panels, above, illustrating the value of <m>w(t)</m> changing with time. </p>

  <p><updateValue name="hideAnimation2" type="boolean" target="$animation2On" newValue="false" hide="not $animation2On">
    <label>Hide animation</label>
  </updateValue>
  <updateValue name="showAnimation2" type="boolean" target="$animation2On" newValue="true" hide="$animation2On">
  <label>Show animation</label>
  </updateValue></p>
</aside>

<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolution3" type="boolean" target="$showSolution3" newValue="true" disabled="$showSolution3">
  <label>show third solution curve</label>
</updateValue> and adjust the curve so that it begins at the initial condition <m>w(0) = 10</m> and starts moving with the right slope.
<answer disabled="not $showSolution3" name="ansSol3Slope1">
  <award referencesAreResponses="$wt.p31y" matchPartial="true">
    <when>$wt.p31y = 10 and -1 &lt; $wt.p31m &lt; -0.2</when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.P31disp
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>

<feedback updateWith="$ansSol3Slope1" condition="$wt.p31y != 10">
  The initial point is not in the correct location.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol3Slope1" condition="$wt.p31y = 10 and $wt.p31m >= 0">
  Given that <m>w(0)=10</m>, the solution must begin by decreasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol3Slope1" condition="$wt.p31y = 10 and -0.2 &lt;= $wt.p31m &lt; 0">
  Increase the magnitude of the initial slope to make it easier to see that the solution begins by decreasing.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol3Slope1" condition="$wt.p31y = 10 and $wt.p31m &lt;= -1">
  Decrease the magnitude of the initial slope.
</feedback>



<p>Move the right point on the curve and set its slope so that the solution curve <m>w(t)</m> has the proper trend as <m>t</m> increases.
<answer disabled="not $showSolution3" name="ansSol3">
  <award>
    <when>
      $wt.p31y = 10 and -1 &lt; $wt.p31m &lt; -0.2
      and $wt.p32y = $c
      and $wt.p32dy = 0
    </when>
  </award>
  <considerAsResponses>
    $wt.P32 $wt.P32disp
  </considerAsResponses>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol3" condition="not ($wt.p31y = 10 and -1 &lt; $wt.p31m &lt; -0.2)">
  The initial point and slope are not correct.  First, set up up the beginning of the curve before adjusting the second point.
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol3" condition="$wt.p31y = 10 and -1 &lt; $wt.p31m &lt; -0.2 and $wt.p32dy > 0">
  Can the solution <m>w(t)</m> keep decreasing to smaller and smaller values?
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol3" condition="$wt.p31y = 10 and -1 &lt; $wt.p31m &lt; -0.2 and $wt.p32dy &lt; 0">
  Can the solution <m>w(t)</m> starting at <m>w(0) = 10</m> turn around and start increasing?
</feedback>
<feedback updateWith="$ansSol3" condition="$wt.p31y = 10 and -1 &lt; $wt.p31m &lt; -0.2 and $wt.p32dy = 0 and $wt.p32y != $c">
  The solution should asymptote by approaching what value?
</feedback>


<aside hide="$ansSol3.creditAchieved &lt; 1" collapsible="false" boxed="true">
  <title>Third solution correct!</title>

  <p>You're unstoppable!  We've started one more animation in both panels, above, illustrating the value of <m>w(t)</m> changing with time. </p>

  <p><updateValue name="hideAnimation3" type="boolean" target="$animation3On" newValue="false" hide="not $animation3On">
    <label>Hide animation</label>
  </updateValue>
  <updateValue name="showAnimation3" type="boolean" target="$animation3On" newValue="true" hide="$animation3On">
  <label>Show animation</label>
  </updateValue></p>
</aside>



<p>Click <updateValue name="revealSolution456" type="boolean" target="$showSolution456" newValue="true" disabled="$showSolution456">
  <label>show last solution curves</label>
</updateValue> and move the points and slopes so that the curves are the solutions for <m>w(0)=$a</m>, <m>w(0)=$b</m>, and <m>w(0)=$c</m>.
<answer disabled="not $showSolution456" name="ansSol456">
  <award matchPartial="true">
    <when>
      <mathList>$wt.p41y $wt.p51y $wt.p61y</mathList>
      = <mathList unordered="true">$a $b $c</mathList>
      and
      <boolean>$wt.p41m = 0 and $wt.p42y = $wt.p41y and $wt.p42dy = 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$wt.p51m = 0 and $wt.p52y = $wt.p51y and $wt.p52dy = 0</boolean>
      and
      <boolean>$wt.p61m = 0 and $wt.p62y = $wt.p61y and $wt.p62dy = 0</boolean>
    </when>
  </award>
</answer>
</p>


<aside hide="$ansSol456.creditAchieved &lt; 1" collapsible="false" boxed="true">
  <title>Last solutions correct!</title>

  <p>You correctly graphed the equilibrium solutions.</p>

  <p>Since the values of <m>w(t)</m> at an equilibrium don't change, an animation would be pretty boring. </p>

</aside>


</section>



<!-- <solution>


<ol>

<li>
Highlight the horizontal axis of the plot of <m>f</m> versus <m>w</m> and plot the point <m>(&#x2D;2,0)</m>.
</li>

<li>
Highlight the vertical axis and plot the point <m>(0,&#x2D;2)</m>.
</li>

<li>
From the formula <m>w'(0)=1&#x2D;w(0)^2/9 = 1&#x2D;($u01 )^2/9 = <round numberdigits="5"><number>$fu01 </number></round></m>.  Since <m>w'(0)</m> is positive, <m>w(t)</m> is increasing at <m>t=0</m>.
</li>

<li>
Draw rightward arrow on plot of <m>f</m> versus <m>w</m>.  Plot the beginning of the green curve, below.
</li>

<li>
As <m>w(t)</m> increases toward <m>&#x2D;1</m>, the velocity increases.  <m>w(t)</m> speeds up.
</li>

<li>
<m>w(t)</m> reaches it maximum speed when <m>w(t)=0</m>.  It slows down to a halt, as <m>w(t)</m> approaches 3.  As <m>t</m> gets large, <m>w(t)</m> approaches 3.  Finish green curve on plot, below.
</li>

<li>
When <m>u_0=4</m>, <m>w(t)</m> decreases toward 3, and approaches 3 for large <m>t</m>.  The plot is the blue curve, below.  When <m>u_0=&#x2D;3.1</m>, <m>w(t)</m> decreases and speeds up, shooting downward off the graph.  The plot is the red curve, below.

